{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"plan","namespace":"","artifact_type":"","name":"P7ac7ec69"},"owner_reference":"#3276","content":"{\"plan_id\":\"P7ac7ec69\",\"feature\":\"contract-verification-async-reporting\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"description\":\"\",\"goal\":\"Make bare Agent()-dispatched dh agents deliver their findings through a durable backend destination instead of a synchronous return value, at both defect sites (contract-verification/implement-feature step 4a, and groom/error.md's Agent Failure procedure), and stop dispatch-contract/SKILL.md from authorizing the anti-pattern that regenerates it.\",\"context\":\"# Plan Context \\u2014 #3276 Contract-Verification Async Reporting\\n\\n## The defect in one paragraph\\n\\nUnder this runtime's dispatch semantics, a bare non-team `Agent()` call returns only a\\n\\\"Spawned successfully\\\" acknowledgment \\u2014 the subagent's plain-text output is never visible to\\nthe dispatcher. Three files encode the opposite assumption. `agents/contract-verification.md:151`\\nsays \\\"Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \\u2014 you register no artifact and\\nwrite no task state.\\\" `skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` lines 187-198 branch on that\\nnever-arriving value. `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` step 3\\ndispatches `dh:task-worker` asking it to \\\"Report: last_success, first_failure, failure_type\\\" and\\nfeeds those into a Mermaid decision tree at lines 30-40 that can therefore never reach its\\nEscalation branch. `skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md:11` is the doctrine that authorizes the\\npattern.\\n\\nThere is no error, no timeout, no blocked state. \\\"No violations found\\\" is byte-for-byte\\nindistinguishable from \\\"the finding was dropped.\\\" That silence is why this is P2.\\n\\n## The fix pattern (same pattern, deliberately different destinations)\\n\\nEach agent writes its own result to a backend destination named in its own agent file; the\\nconsumer reads it back. Per `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<result_destination>` primary\\nrule \\u2014 \\\"Put your result where your own agent file says\\\" \\u2014 this resolves routing before the\\nartifact fallback is ever reached.\\n\\nThe two sites use different destinations because their consumption shapes differ:\\n\\n- **contract-verification \\u2192 `backlog_groom` on the Concerns section.** Accumulating, human-facing,\\n  actionable checklist items, consumed much later by `/complete-implementation`. Same shape as\\n  every other concern already flowing into that section. Architect \\u00a71.1 explicitly rejects\\n  `artifact_register` here: no `artifact_type` enum member fits, and `code-review` is already\\n  owned by `dh:code-reviewer`.\\n- **groom/error.md diagnostic \\u2192 `artifact_register`.** Single-shot structured diagnostic data\\n  feeding one immediate decision \\u2014 the T0/TN shape, not the checklist shape.\\n\\nForcing one mechanism onto both sites would be the over-generalization Impact Radius R5 warns\\nagainst.\\n\\n## Hard exclusions (all four are traps this item has already walked into)\\n\\n1. **Never `TeamCreate`.** Explicitly ruled out by the user. Both sites stay bare `Agent()` spawns.\\n2. **Never `SendMessage(to=\\\"team-lead\\\", ...)`.** There is no team-lead when there is no team. This\\n   is #3249's fix for `impact-analyst` and it is NOT transferable \\u2014 `impact-analyst` is a\\n   `TeamCreate` teammate. It is the most discoverable wrong answer.\\n3. **Never re-introduce SendMessage as transport at all.** Commit `11082f0eb` removed it\\n   deliberately: SendMessage is Claude-Code-only, and this plugin's content also targets Codex,\\n   OpenCode, Crush and Kilo.\\n4. **Never touch #3250's scope.** #3250 audits TeamCreate teammates missing a\\n   SendMessage-to-team-lead step. The two items must remain independently verifiable\\n   (Acceptance Criterion 5).\\n\\n## Files, and only these files\\n\\n| File | Task | Change |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` | T1 | New `issue_number` input; Output Format rewritten to `backlog_groom`; drop unused `SendMessage` grant |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` | T1 | Add `Issue number: {issue}` to step 4a's dispatch prompt; delete the synchronous branch at lines 187-198 |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` | T2 | Diagnostic agent registers a YAML `audit-report` artifact; read-back inserted before the decision tree |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` | T3 | Split the conflated sentence at line 11; keep specialist-selection, delete the reporting claim |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` | T4 | **VERIFY UNCHANGED.** Architect \\u00a75 confirms no edit needed |\\n\\n## Explicitly deferred (architect \\u00a77 \\u2014 do not fold in)\\n\\n- A general documentation-and-review gate requiring all `dh:*` agents to declare a completion-report\\n  step, plus a follow-up audit of the remaining ~4 agents named in the frequency analysis. Not in\\n  this item's five Acceptance Criteria; belongs in a separate follow-up item.\\n- `skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 independently verified clean (zero bare `Agent(` calls,\\n  already artifact-register-based). Correct pattern. Do not touch.\\n\\n## Why T1 merges the producer and the consumer\\n\\nThe orchestrator's decomposition brief offered the choice. They are merged because splitting them\\nproduces an incorrect intermediate state in either order: land the agent edit alone and BOTH the\\nagent and step 4a write the concern (duplicate entries); land the step 4a edit alone and nothing\\nwrites at all (the original defect, now with the fallback removed). Architect \\u00a72.2 notes they can\\nland in one commit. They are two halves of one destination move against a single defect site, so\\nthey are one task.\\n\\n## Why T3 does not depend on T1 or T2\\n\\nThe doctrine change cross-references `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<result_destination>` clause,\\nwhich already exists unchanged in the working tree. It does not quote or depend on any prose T1 or\\nT2 writes. T1, T2 and T3 are fully parallel.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"\",\"issue\":\"3276\",\"tasks\":[{\"id\":\"T1\",\"title\":\"Route contract-verification findings to the Concerns section, and stop implement-feature step 4a from reading a return value\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"dh:task-worker\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"skills\":[\"plugin-creator:claude-plugins-reference-2026\"],\"created\":\"2026-08-28T18:34:23.302149+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"Two files, one atomic change. `agents/contract-verification.md` currently ends its Output Format section with a synchronous-return override (line 151: \\\"Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \\u2014 you register no artifact and write no task state.\\\"). `skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` lines 187-198 branch on that never-arriving value. The producer gains the write; the consumer loses the read.\\n\\nThese are merged into one task deliberately: landing either half alone yields an incorrect intermediate state \\u2014 the agent edit alone causes BOTH the agent and step 4a to write each concern (duplicates), the step 4a edit alone leaves nothing writing at all. Architect \\u00a72.2 confirms they can land in one commit.\\n\\nProse only. No Python, no schema changes, no new tools.\",\"objective\":\"Move responsibility for recording contract findings from the orchestrator (which reads a value that never arrives) to the contract-verification agent itself (which writes directly to the backlog item's Concerns section before it returns), and remove the now-dead consumer branch.\",\"requirements\":\"### File A \\u2014 `plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md`\\n\\n**A1. Frontmatter (line 5).** Change `tools:` to exactly:\\n\\n```\\ntools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Skill, mcp__plugin_dh_sam, mcp__plugin_dh_backlog\\n```\\n\\nThat is the current list minus `SendMessage`. Nothing else in the frontmatter changes. Rationale (architect \\u00a71.4): no instruction uses the grant after this change, and an unused SendMessage grant is exactly what invites the next author to reach for the wrong precedent.\\n\\n**A2. Inputs section (currently lines 23-32).** The section currently opens \\\"You receive three inputs in your delegation prompt\\\" and lists `architect_spec_path`, `task_id`, `modified_files`. Make it four, adding:\\n\\n- `issue_number` \\u2014 the parent backlog item's identifier (`str | int` \\u2014 GitHub integer ID or beads nanoid string), used to address `backlog_groom`.\\n\\nUpdate the \\\"three inputs\\\" count in the prose. Extend the existing BLOCKED sentence so a missing `issue_number` is explicitly a BLOCKED condition, mirroring how `agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` treats its own required `item_id`.\\n\\n**A3. Output Format section (lines 121-151) \\u2014 replace the delivery mechanism only.** Steps 1-5 (lines 34-119, extraction and comparison) are NOT part of this defect and must be left byte-identical. Within Output Format:\\n\\n- The `<concerns>` block structure at lines 127-139 and the \\\"Each concern entry must include\\\" list at 141-145 describe how a finding is *shaped*. Keep that shaping guidance; it now describes the content the agent assembles before writing rather than text it returns.\\n- **Delete line 151 entirely** (`Your finding is your response...`).\\n- Add a delivery procedure that issues **exactly one `backlog_groom` call per finding, and exactly one call when there are no findings \\u2014 never zero calls**:\\n  - Per violation:\\n    ```\\n    mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(\\n        selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\",\\n        section=\\\"Concerns\\\",\\n        content=\\\"- [ ] CONTRACT: {severity} \\u2014 {issue line, one sentence} (reported by contract-verification on {task_id})\\\",\\n        append=True\\n    )\\n    ```\\n    This is the exact content shape `implement-feature/SKILL.md` builds today at lines 190-196 \\u2014 moved into the producer, not redesigned.\\n  - When clean, one call with a pre-resolved entry:\\n    ```\\n    content=\\\"- [x] CONTRACT: no concerns \\u2014 all contracts in scope satisfied (reported by contract-verification on {task_id})\\\"\\n    ```\\n    The leading `[x]` versus `[ ]` is the interface contract distinguishing a clean run from a dropped one at a glance. This is what makes Acceptance Criterion 2 true.\\n- **Terminal response.** End with `STATUS: DONE` on its own first line (per `dh:subagent-contract`'s `<status>` clause, a skill this agent already loads), summarizing what was written \\u2014 e.g. `Wrote N contract finding(s) to #{issue_number} Concerns section (task {task_id}).` The response text is no longer load-bearing: the write lands before the agent returns.\\n\\n**A4. Operating Rules (lines 153-162).** The rule \\\"If the architect spec has no Component Design or Type System Design section, output `No contract concerns \\u2014 no contracts defined in spec`\\\" now describes a return value on a channel nobody reads. Route that case through the same clean-result `backlog_groom` call as A3, so it too is affirmatively recorded rather than silent.\\n\\n### File B \\u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md`\\n\\n**B1. Dispatch prompt (lines 166-185).** Add one line to the delegation prompt body, alongside the existing `Task ID:` / `Plan:` / `Architect spec:` lines:\\n\\n```\\nIssue number: {issue}\\n```\\n\\n`{issue}` is the same local name section 4 already uses at line 141 (`selector=\\\"#{issue}\\\"`), and step 4a already runs inside the `if a parent issue number is known` branch at line 151 \\u2014 the value is in scope at dispatch time. No new plumbing.\\n\\n**B2. Delete lines 187-198 in full** \\u2014 the paragraph beginning \\\"If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block\\\", the `backlog_groom` code fence that follows it, and its trailing \\\"Use the MCP tool for this call.\\\" line. That responsibility now lives in the agent (A3).\\n\\n**B3. Preserve line 200 unchanged**: \\\"If `artifact_read` fails or returns no content (no architect spec for this issue), skip step 4a entirely.\\\" That is a pre-dispatch skip condition, unrelated to result routing.\\n\\n**B4. Nothing else in step 4a changes.** No wait loop, no poll, no read-back. Architect \\u00a72.3: nothing in step 4a consumes the concerns immediately \\u2014 line 149 already states they feed `/complete-implementation`, a materially later step, and the existing TN bookend plus Completion Gate (lines 346-349) already provide the ordering guarantee. Proceed to step 4b once the dispatch call returns.\",\"constraints\":\"- **No `TeamCreate`.** contract-verification stays a bare background `Agent()` spawn. Explicitly excluded by the user.\\n- **No `SendMessage`, in any form** \\u2014 not `to=\\\"team-lead\\\"`, not to anyone. There is no team-lead in this dispatch context, and commit `11082f0eb` removed SendMessage deliberately because it is Claude-Code-only while this plugin also targets Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Kilo. Re-adding it would undo that portability work.\\n- **No `artifact_register` for this site.** Architect \\u00a71.1 rejects it with three reasons: no `artifact_type` enum member names per-task contract findings; `code-review` is already owned by `dh:code-reviewer`'s S6 schema and reusing it would corrupt type-alone reads; and Acceptance Criteria 1-2 name Concerns as the destination directly.\\n- **Do not touch Steps 1-5** of contract-verification.md (lines 34-119). Extraction and comparison logic is out of scope.\\n- **Do not touch `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`** \\u2014 architect \\u00a75 confirms it needs no change; its primary rule (\\\"Put your result where your own agent file says\\\") is what makes this fix legal.\\n- **Do not touch `skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md`** \\u2014 verified clean, already correct.\\n- **Do not extend the artifact_type enum** \\u2014 that is a Python/backend change, out of scope for this markdown-only item.\\n- Both files are agent-facing instruction prose. Follow the plugin's existing voice and formatting; do not restructure sections beyond what is specified.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"Two modified files:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \\u2014 frontmatter tools list, Inputs section, Output Format section, and one Operating Rules bullet.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 one added line in step 4a's dispatch prompt, one deleted block (lines 187-198).\\n\\nNo new files. No Python. No schema changes.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. `plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` frontmatter `tools:` line reads exactly `tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Skill, mcp__plugin_dh_sam, mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` \\u2014 `SendMessage` appears nowhere in the file.\\n2. The Inputs section lists four inputs including `issue_number` with its `str | int` type note, and a missing `issue_number` is stated as a BLOCKED condition.\\n3. The string `finding is your response` does not appear anywhere in the file.\\n4. The Output Format section instructs a `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom` call with `section=\\\"Concerns\\\"` and `append=True` for the violations case, using the `- [ ] CONTRACT: ...(reported by contract-verification on {task_id})` content shape.\\n5. The Output Format section instructs a `backlog_groom` call for the no-violations case using a `- [x] CONTRACT: no concerns` content shape \\u2014 so the clean path issues exactly one call, never zero.\\n6. The agent's terminal response is specified to begin with `STATUS: DONE` as its own first line.\\n7. `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a's delegation prompt includes an issue-number line passing `{issue}` to the agent.\\n8. The paragraph beginning `If the contract-verification agent returns a` and its following `backlog_groom` code fence are deleted from `implement-feature/SKILL.md`.\\n9. Step 4a's pre-dispatch skip condition (\\\"If `artifact_read` fails or returns no content... skip step 4a entirely\\\") is still present and unmodified.\\n10. Lines 34-119 of contract-verification.md (Contract Extraction Process through Step 5 Scope Narrowing) are byte-identical to their pre-change content.\\n11. Neither file gained any line containing `TeamCreate`, `team-lead`, or `SendMessage`.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `grep -n 'SendMessage\\\\|TeamCreate\\\\|team-lead' plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect no output from contract-verification.md; any hit in implement-feature/SKILL.md must be pre-existing (confirm with `git diff` that it is not an added line).\\n2. `grep -c 'finding is your response' plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \\u2014 expect `0`.\\n3. `grep -n 'issue_number' plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \\u2014 expect hits in both the Inputs section and the Output Format `backlog_groom` call.\\n4. `grep -n 'backlog_groom' plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \\u2014 expect the violations-case call and the clean-case call to be distinguishable, one carrying `- [ ] CONTRACT:` and one carrying `- [x] CONTRACT:`.\\n5. `grep -n 'If the contract-verification agent returns' plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect no output.\\n6. `grep -n 'Issue number\\\\|{issue}' plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 confirm the dispatch prompt at step 4a now passes the issue number.\\n7. `git diff plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \\u2014 read the full hunk list and confirm no hunk touches lines within the Contract Extraction Process or Verification Process sections (Steps 1-5).\\n8. `git diff plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \\u2014 confirm the diff contains exactly one addition (the issue-number line) and one deletion block (lines 187-198), and nothing else.\\n\\n**CoVe check \\u2014 answer each in writing before marking this task complete:**\\n\\n- **Q1.** Does the clean path issue exactly one `backlog_groom` call, never zero? Quote the instruction that makes zero calls impossible. If the file can be read as \\\"say nothing when clean,\\\" Acceptance Criterion 2 is not met and the defect survives in a new costume.\\n- **Q2.** Trace `{issue}` from `implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a's dispatch prompt into `contract-verification.md`'s `backlog_groom(selector=\\\"#{issue_number}\\\")`. Name the line in each file. If either end is missing, the agent will BLOCK on every run.\\n- **Q3.** After deleting lines 187-198, is there any remaining instruction anywhere in `implement-feature/SKILL.md` that reads the contract-verification agent's textual output? Grep and confirm. A surviving reader means a duplicate-write or a dead branch.\\n- **Q4.** Confirm exactly one writer exists per concern \\u2014 the agent \\u2014 and step 4a writes none. Two writers means duplicate Concerns entries on every run.\\n- **Q5.** Re-read the Constraints list. Confirm each of the four hard exclusions (TeamCreate, SendMessage, artifact_register, subagent-contract edits) is respected, citing the evidence for each.\",\"context_notes\":\"Root doctrine: `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<result_destination>` clause \\u2014 \\\"Put your result where your own agent file says\\\" \\u2014 is the primary rule this fix satisfies by naming Concerns explicitly in the agent file. The artifact fallback (\\\"every other document is an artifact\\\") activates only where no agent file names a destination, which is why it does not apply here.\\n\\nWorking precedent for a bare-`Agent()` agent routing results through the backend in this same plugin: `agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` and `agents/tn-verification-gate.md` (both use `artifact_register`; the mechanism differs, the discipline is identical).\\n\\nHistory worth knowing: commit `11082f0eb` removed contract-verification's SendMessage step for sound cross-harness portability reasons but substituted \\\"your finding is your response\\\" \\u2014 a channel that does not exist under async dispatch. Commit `43058f345` fixed the sibling symptom for `impact-analyst` with `SendMessage(to=\\\"team-lead\\\")`, which works there only because impact-analyst is a `TeamCreate` teammate. Copying it here would reproduce the defect.\",\"issue_classification\":\"defect\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"high\"},{\"id\":\"T2\",\"title\":\"Route groom/error.md's failure diagnostic through artifact_register and read it back before the decision tree\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"dh:task-worker\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"skills\":[\"plugin-creator:claude-plugins-reference-2026\"],\"created\":\"2026-08-28T18:34:23.302427+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"Second, independent defect site. Same root assumption as T1, different file, different consumption shape, therefore a different destination.\\n\\n`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` step 3 (lines 15-26) dispatches `dh:task-worker` via a bare `Agent()` call ending in \\\"Report: last_success, first_failure, failure_type (interrupted | tool_error | unknown)\\\". Step 4's Mermaid flowchart (lines 30-40) branches on `failure_type`, and its `2nd failure? --> Yes --> Escalate` edge is currently dead code guarding an escalation path \\u2014 the value it branches on never arrives.\\n\\nUnlike T1's accumulating checklist, this is single-shot structured diagnostic data consumed once, immediately, by the same workflow that produced it. That is the `t0-baseline-capture` / `tn-verification-gate` shape, so `artifact_register` is the correct destination here (architect \\u00a73.1).\\n\\nProse only, one file.\",\"objective\":\"Make the Agent Failure procedure's Mermaid decision tree branch on real diagnostic data by having the dispatched diagnostic agent register its findings as an artifact and having the procedure read that artifact back before branching \\u2014 turning a currently-unreachable Escalation path into a live one.\",\"requirements\":\"All changes are to `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md`, section `### 1. Agent Failure`.\\n\\n**R1. Thread the item reference into the diagnostic dispatch (step 3, lines 17-26).** The delegation prompt currently carries no item identifier. Add the existing `<item_ref/>` placeholder \\u2014 already used elsewhere in this same file (line 51, `backlog update --selector \\\"<item_ref/>\\\"`), so no new input needs threading into the file, only into the prompt.\\n\\n**R2. Replace the \\\"Report:\\\" line with a register instruction.** Line 24 currently reads `Report: last_success, first_failure, failure_type (interrupted | tool_error | unknown)`. Replace it with an instruction to assemble this YAML in memory (no file write \\u2014 same discipline as T0/TN) and register it:\\n\\n```yaml\\ndiagnosed_at: \\\"<ISO 8601 UTC>\\\"\\nstage: \\\"<the step/stage the failed agent was executing>\\\"\\nlast_success: \\\"<last successful tool call or output, or null>\\\"\\nfirst_failure: \\\"<first error, timeout, or missing output, or null>\\\"\\nfailure_type: \\\"interrupted | tool_error | unknown\\\"\\n```\\n\\nRegistered via:\\n\\n```text\\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_register(\\n    item_id=<item_ref/>,\\n    artifact_type=\\\"audit-report\\\",\\n    artifact_id=\\\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\\\",\\n    content=<yaml_string>,\\n    agent=\\\"task-worker\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nThe `artifact_id` is a **fixed string, never timestamp-suffixed**. `artifact_register` is idempotent by `(artifact_type, artifact_id)`, so a retry's diagnostic overwrites the previous one in place \\u2014 which is the desired behavior, since the procedure only ever needs the latest diagnostic to drive its immediate next branch, never a history.\\n\\n**R3. Insert a read-back between step 3 and step 4.** Immediately after the dispatch block ends (line 26) and before `4. Based on the diagnostic:` (line 28), add an explicit read-back step:\\n\\n```text\\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_read(\\n    item_id=<item_ref/>,\\n    artifact_type=\\\"audit-report\\\",\\n    artifact_id=\\\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nParse the returned YAML for `last_success`, `first_failure`, and `failure_type`. State explicitly that the `failure_type` the decision tree branches on is the parsed field from this read-back.\\n\\n**The `artifact_id` argument is mandatory on this read and must not be omitted** \\u2014 see the Constraints note on shared type ownership. A type-alone read returns whichever producer of `audit-report` wrote most recently.\\n\\n**R4. Route a missing artifact to the file's existing System Error procedure.** If `artifact_read` returns no content \\u2014 the diagnostic agent failed to register, or was itself interrupted before registering \\u2014 route to this same file's `### 3. System Error` procedure (lines 76-105) rather than guessing a `failure_type`. This reuses an already-specified escape hatch in the same file; do not invent a new one.\\n\\n**R5. Leave the Mermaid flowchart (lines 30-40) structurally unchanged.** It already branches on a variable named `failure_type`. Only that variable's source changes \\u2014 from \\\"the diagnostic agent's return value\\\" to \\\"the parsed field from the read-back.\\\" Do not redraw, re-label, or restructure the chart.\\n\\n**R6. Note the shared `audit-report` ownership.** `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md:161` currently attributes `audit-report` solely to `doc-drift-auditor`, and `agents/code-reviewer.md:180` describes it as \\\"reserved for `dh:doc-drift-auditor`\\\". This task adds a second producer under a distinct `artifact_id`. Update the AGENTS.md:161 table row to name both producers so the ownership documentation stays truthful. That single row is the only edit permitted outside `error.md`; if it cannot be made accurately in one line, stop and report rather than expanding scope.\",\"constraints\":\"- **No `TeamCreate`.** The diagnostic stays a bare background `Agent()` spawn.\\n- **No `SendMessage`**, in any form or to any recipient.\\n- **Do not restructure the Mermaid flowchart.** Its `Q{2nd failure?} -->|Yes| Escalate` edge must survive verbatim \\u2014 proving it is now reachable is Acceptance Criterion 4's entire point.\\n- **Do not use `backlog_groom` here.** T1's Concerns destination is deliberately not reused: this is single-shot data feeding one immediate decision, not an accumulating human-facing checklist. Architect's ground rule is same pattern, destination chosen per consumption shape.\\n- **Never read `audit-report` by type alone in this file.** Always pass `artifact_id=\\\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\\\"`. `doc-drift-auditor` also writes `audit-report` (under `doc-drift-audit-{slug}`), and a type-alone read returns the most recent of the two.\\n- **Do not extend the `artifact_type` enum.** `audit-report` is an existing member (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:1582`); a new member would be a Python change, out of scope.\\n- **Do not touch the other error categories** (Workflow Block, System Error, Escalation) beyond the single cross-reference added by R4.\\n- Known ceiling, acceptable: the fixed `artifact_id` gives one diagnostic slot per item. Concurrent grooming diagnostics for the same item would collide. The grooming workflow is single-threaded per item, so this is not reachable today; do not build per-dispatch uniqueness for it.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 modified step 3 dispatch prompt, new read-back step between 3 and 4, unchanged Mermaid chart.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md` \\u2014 one table row updated (`audit-report` producers).\\n\\nNo new files. No Python. No schema changes.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. `groom/error.md` step 3's delegation prompt includes the `<item_ref/>` placeholder, so the diagnostic agent knows which item to register against.\\n2. The literal string `Report: last_success, first_failure, failure_type` no longer appears in the file.\\n3. The file contains an `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_register` call specifying `artifact_type=\\\"audit-report\\\"` and the fixed `artifact_id=\\\"audit-report-groom-agent-failure\\\"`.\\n4. The registered content schema is documented in the file as YAML with all five keys: `diagnosed_at`, `stage`, `last_success`, `first_failure`, `failure_type`.\\n5. An `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_read` call appears in the file positioned between the step-3 dispatch block and the step-4 decision tree, and it passes `artifact_id` explicitly.\\n6. The file states that `failure_type` (the Mermaid chart's branch variable) is parsed from the read-back result.\\n7. The file routes an empty/absent `artifact_read` result to its own `### 3. System Error` procedure by name \\u2014 not to a guessed `failure_type`, not to a newly invented handler.\\n8. The Mermaid flowchart at lines 30-40 is unchanged: all node ids (`Diag`, `Resume`, `ToolErr`, `Retry`, `Q`, `Escalate`, `Continue`, `SysErr`) and all edges are byte-identical to their pre-change form.\\n9. `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md`'s `audit-report` table row names both producers.\\n10. No line containing `TeamCreate`, `team-lead`, or `SendMessage` was added to any file.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `grep -n 'artifact_register\\\\|artifact_read\\\\|item_ref' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 confirm the register call sits inside the step-3 dispatch prompt and the read call sits after it and before step 4, by line number.\\n2. `grep -c 'Report: last_success' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 expect `0`.\\n3. `grep -n 'audit-report-groom-agent-failure' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 expect exactly two hits (the register and the read), both with the identical literal id.\\n4. `git diff plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 confirm no hunk falls inside the ```mermaid fence.\\n5. `sed -n '/```mermaid/,/```/p'` on the file, compared against `git show HEAD:<same path>` piped through the same extraction \\u2014 expect an empty diff.\\n6. `grep -n 'System Error' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 confirm the new empty-read-back path references it, and that the section itself still exists at its heading.\\n7. `grep -n 'audit-report' plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md` \\u2014 confirm the ownership row names both producers.\\n8. `git diff | grep -E '^\\\\+.*(TeamCreate|team-lead|SendMessage)'` \\u2014 expect no output.\\n\\n**CoVe check \\u2014 answer each in writing before marking this task complete:**\\n\\n- **Q1.** Walk the `Q{2nd failure?} -->|Yes| Escalate` edge backwards to its data source. Name the exact line that now supplies `failure_type`. If you cannot name a line, Acceptance Criterion 4 is not met and the branch is still dead.\\n- **Q2.** The diagnostic agent is interrupted before it calls `artifact_register`. Trace what the procedure does. Confirm it lands in System Error and does not silently branch on an unset `failure_type` \\u2014 that would be the same silent-failure class this whole item exists to eliminate.\\n- **Q3.** `doc-drift-auditor` registered an `audit-report` for this same item earlier in its lifecycle. Does the read-back in step 4 return the drift report or the failure diagnostic? Quote the `artifact_id` argument that decides it. If the read is type-alone anywhere, fix it.\\n- **Q4.** Confirm the Mermaid chart is byte-identical (verification step 5). If any node or edge changed, explain why \\u2014 architect \\u00a73.3 says only the variable's source changes, nothing else.\\n- **Q5.** Confirm this task used `artifact_register` and NOT `backlog_groom`, and state in one sentence why this site's destination differs from T1's. If you cannot articulate the difference, re-read the plan context before proceeding.\",\"context_notes\":\"`audit-report` is an existing `ArtifactType` enum member \\u2014 `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:1582` (`AUDIT_REPORT = \\\"audit-report\\\"`). It is the closest existing member to \\\"diagnostic review of a failed agent's session\\\" and is the reason no enum extension is needed.\\n\\nThe idempotency-by-`(artifact_type, artifact_id)` property is what makes the fixed id correct rather than lazy: a retry's diagnostic replaces the stale one, and the procedure only ever consults the latest.\\n\\nPrecedent for the register-then-read-back shape in this same plugin: `agents/t0-baseline-capture.md` writes the T0 artifact; `agents/tn-verification-gate.md` reads it back and branches. Read those two if the pattern is unclear.\\n## CORRECTION \\u2014 destination changed to Context section, not audit-report/artifact_register\\n\\n**Supersedes R2, R3, R6, and the acceptance-criteria/verification-steps items that reference `artifact_register`, `artifact_read`, or `audit-report-groom-agent-failure` above.** This task was decomposed against an intermediate architect-spec draft. The final, converged architect spec (independently reached by two separate design passes, cross-verified against `AGENTS.md:161`) uses a different destination for this site. Follow this correction, not the superseded text above.\\n\\n**Why the change**: `AGENTS.md:161` reserves `artifact_type=\\\"audit-report\\\"` exclusively to `doc-drift-auditor` (\\\"Never used for a code review verdict\\\"). Extending it to a second producer (as R6 above proposed) requires editing `AGENTS.md` \\u2014 a 6th file outside the 5-file scope the backlog item was groomed and RT-ICA-approved against (`contract-verification.md`, `implement-feature/SKILL.md`, `groom/error.md`, `dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`, `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`). The corrected design avoids the conflict entirely and needs no scope expansion.\\n\\n**New destination: `backlog_groom` on the item's `Context` section** \\u2014 not `Concerns` (would spawn spurious follow-up items downstream, per `implement-feature/SKILL.md:149`) and not `artifact_register`/`audit-report` (registry-reserved, per above).\\n\\n**R2-corrected.** Replace the `Report:` line (originally line 24) with an instruction to assemble and write a fixed-format diagnostic line via:\\n\\n```text\\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(\\n    selector=\\\"<item_ref/>\\\",\\n    section=\\\"Context\\\",\\n    append=True,\\n    content=\\\"DIAGNOSTIC ({ISO8601 timestamp}): stage={stage}; last_success={last_success}; first_failure={first_failure}; failure_type={failure_type} (task-worker diagnostic)\\\"\\n)\\n```\\n\\nThen return `STATUS: DONE` as the final line, per `subagent-contract`.\\n\\n**R3-corrected \\u2014 completion detection before read-back.** Before reading back, reuse this same file's own existing wait-for-idle pattern (the session-health-check Mermaid flowchart already present at lines 120-134 \\u2014 spawn \\u2192 check verdict via `ListAgents`/transcript-analyst \\u2192 wait/re-check on a bounded schedule). Do not invent a new synchronization primitive; apply the existing one to this dispatch.\\n\\n**R3-corrected \\u2014 read-back.** After the wait confirms the diagnostic agent is idle, read back via:\\n\\n```text\\nmcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=\\\"<item_ref/>\\\", section=\\\"Context\\\", show=\\\"last\\\")\\n```\\n\\nParse `failure_type` from the most recent `DIAGNOSTIC (...)` line. This is the value the Mermaid tree (unchanged, per original R5) branches on.\\n\\n**R6 is VOID.** Do not touch `AGENTS.md`. No second `audit-report` producer is being registered, so there is no ownership row to update. If any change to `AGENTS.md` seems necessary, stop and report rather than proceeding \\u2014 that would mean this correction was misapplied.\\n\\n**Corrected acceptance criteria (replace originals 3-5, 9):**\\n- The file contains a `backlog_groom` call with `section=\\\"Context\\\"`, `append=True`, and a `DIAGNOSTIC (...)` content template carrying `stage`, `last_success`, `first_failure`, `failure_type`.\\n- Completion detection before the read-back reuses the existing lines 120-134 wait-for-idle pattern \\u2014 no new polling mechanism is introduced.\\n- A `backlog_view(section=\\\"Context\\\", show=\\\"last\\\")` call appears between the step-3 dispatch block and the step-4 decision tree, and the file states `failure_type` is parsed from its most recent `DIAGNOSTIC` line.\\n- `AGENTS.md` carries zero changes from this task.\\n\\n**Corrected verification steps (replace originals 1, 3, 7, 9):**\\n1. `grep -n 'backlog_groom\\\\|backlog_view\\\\|DIAGNOSTIC' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 confirm the write call, the read call, and the DIAGNOSTIC template are all present, by line number.\\n2. `grep -c 'artifact_register\\\\|artifact_read\\\\|audit-report' plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` \\u2014 expect `0` (the superseded mechanism must not appear in the final file).\\n3. `git diff --quiet main -- plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md; echo \\\"exit=$?\\\"` \\u2014 expect `exit=0` (R6 void \\u2014 zero changes).\\n\\n**Corrected CoVe Q3 and Q5:**\\n- **Q3 (corrected).** Since this no longer uses a shared-type artifact registry slot, the original doc-drift-auditor collision question is moot \\u2014 instead confirm: does the `backlog_view(section=\\\"Context\\\")` read-back correctly isolate the most recent `DIAGNOSTIC` line from any other Context content (e.g. grooming notes) already in that section? Quote the parsing approach.\\n- **Q5 (corrected).** State in one sentence why this site's destination (Context, via `backlog_groom`) differs from T1's (Concerns, also via `backlog_groom`) even though both now use the same tool \\u2014 the distinction is section, not mechanism, unlike the original (now-superseded) artifact_register-based design.\\n## CORRECTION 2 \\u2014 the wait-for-idle citation in CORRECTION 1 was fabricated; replaced with a verified mechanism\\n\\n**CORRECTION 1's R3-corrected cited \\\"this same file's own existing wait-for-idle pattern (the session-health-check Mermaid flowchart already present at lines 120-134)\\\" as the completion-detection mechanism to reuse. That citation is false and must not be followed.** Verified directly by reading `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md` in full (126 lines): lines 120-125 are the **Escalation** section (report to user, set status blocked, create a backlog item) \\u2014 there is no wait-for-idle logic, no `ListAgents` usage, no `transcript-analyst` reference, and no Mermaid flowchart resembling a session-health check anywhere in this file. A plugin-wide grep for `ListAgents`, `idle_notification`, `wait-for-idle`, `session-health-check`, and `transcript-analyst` across all of `plugins/development-harness/` returns zero hits. No such pattern exists anywhere in this codebase to reuse. Disregard CORRECTION 1's R3-corrected citation entirely; the rest of CORRECTION 1 (the `backlog_groom`/Context-section destination, the DIAGNOSTIC content template, R6-void) stands.\\n\\n**Replacement completion-detection design**, grounded in this runtime's actual, directly-observed dispatch behavior (not a citation to file content): a bare `Agent()` dispatch is asynchronous \\u2014 the orchestrator that dispatches it does not block, and later receives an automatic notification (an idle/completion event) when the dispatched agent finishes, delivered to the orchestrator's own session without any polling call. This is the same mechanism this plan's own decomposition already relies on implicitly (T1/architect \\u00a72.3's \\\"whatever async acknowledgment or idle notification the runtime supplies\\\"). Apply it explicitly here, since (unlike T1) T2's Mermaid tree needs the value immediately after dispatch, not eventually:\\n\\n1. After dispatching the diagnostic agent (step 3), the orchestrator running this groom workflow waits for that dispatch's completion/idle notification before proceeding \\u2014 this is passive (the runtime delivers it automatically), not an active poll loop, and requires no new tool call or synchronization primitive.\\n2. Once the notification arrives, perform the read-back: `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=\\\"<item_ref/>\\\", section=\\\"Context\\\", show=\\\"last\\\")`.\\n3. **The read-back result, not the notification itself, is the actual completion signal for content.** A completion notification only means the diagnostic agent stopped running \\u2014 it does not by itself guarantee the `backlog_groom` write happened (an interrupted or failing diagnostic agent can still go idle without writing). If the most recent `Context` entry is not a `DIAGNOSTIC (...)` line matching the expected template (or the section has no qualifying entry at all), treat this as the diagnostic agent having failed to register \\u2014 route to this file's own `### 3. System Error` procedure per R4, exactly as already specified. Do not guess a `failure_type` from a missing or malformed read-back.\\n\\nThis keeps the CORRECTION 1 instruction \\\"do not invent a new synchronization primitive\\\" honestly true: nothing new is added beyond what this runtime already does for every bare `Agent()` dispatch, and the R4 System Error fallback (already specified) is what actually guards against a write that never lands \\u2014 not a wait-for-idle flowchart that doesn't exist.\",\"issue_classification\":\"defect\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"high\"},{\"id\":\"T3\",\"title\":\"Split dispatch-contract's conflated sentence: keep specialist selection, delete the 'finding is its response' reporting claim\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"dh:task-worker\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":2,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"plugin-creator:claude-plugins-reference-2026\"],\"created\":\"2026-08-28T18:34:23.302533+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"`skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md:11-12` currently reads:\\n\\n> A dispatch that hands over no dh operation \\u2014 an independent review whose finding is its response \\u2014\\n> has nothing here to reach: any specialist that fits runs it, dh roster or not.\\n\\nOne sentence carrying two independent claims. The first \\u2014 that a review-only dispatch needs no `dh:` specialist or `dh:task-worker` fallback, because there is no SAM/artifact operation only a dh-aware agent could perform \\u2014 is true, on-topic for a file whose stated subject is \\\"which agent a dh workflow dispatches\\\", and must survive intact.\\n\\nThe second \\u2014 \\\"whose finding is its response\\\" \\u2014 is an incorrect claim about *where results go*, smuggled into a sentence about *agent selection*. It is the doctrine `contract-verification.md:151` was written against. Per Root-Cause Analysis this pattern recurs across 6 agents; leaving the phrase standing regenerates the defect for the next author no matter how many individual agents get fixed (Impact Radius R2).\\n\\nOne file, roughly one sentence. This is the smallest diff in the plan and the one with the longest half-life.\",\"objective\":\"Stop the doctrine file from authorizing the anti-pattern that produced both defect sites, without weakening the true specialist-selection guidance packed into the same sentence.\",\"requirements\":\"All changes are to `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`, `<dispatch_selection>` block (lines 9-20).\\n\\n**R1. Split the sentence at lines 11-12 into its two independent claims.**\\n\\n**R2. Keep the specialist-selection claim, standing alone.** A dispatch handing over no dh operation does not require a `dh:` specialist or the `dh:task-worker` fallback \\u2014 any specialist that fits runs it, dh roster or not. This clause is true, on-topic, and orthogonal to the defect (architect \\u00a74.1). Do not weaken, hedge, or delete it.\\n\\n**R3. Delete the reporting claim.** The phrase \\\"whose finding is its response\\\" must not survive in any form that can be read as naming a result destination.\\n\\n**R4. Replace the deleted claim with an explicit cross-reference, not a silent gap.** Add an adjacent clause stating that regardless of which specialist is chosen, where the finding goes is never decided by this file \\u2014 it is governed by `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<result_destination>` clause, every time, including for review-only dispatches. A silent deletion leaves the next author with the same unanswered question that produced the defect.\\n\\n**R5. If the phrase \\\"has nothing here to reach\\\" survives, re-scope it unambiguously.** It may only mean \\\"no dh roster requirement\\\" \\u2014 never \\\"no destination requirement.\\\" If that disambiguation cannot be made cleanly in the surrounding prose, rewrite the clause instead of keeping the phrase.\\n\\n**R6. Leave `<artifact_consumption>` (lines 22-27) untouched.** Architect \\u00a74.3: it is orthogonal to this defect. It is already the invariant T2's read-back satisfies, and T1 deliberately does not violate it (Concerns entries are not artifacts, so the clause does not apply to them and must not be stretched to cover them).\\n\\n**R7. Leave the frontmatter unchanged.** The `description` field \\u2014 \\\"Which agent a dh workflow dispatches, and the artifact consumer invariant\\\" \\u2014 is already accurate and already scopes the file to selection, which is precisely the framing this edit restores.\\n\\nExact prose is an implementation decision. Match the file's existing terse, declarative voice \\u2014 it is 27 lines total, and every sentence is load-bearing.\",\"constraints\":\"- **Do not edit `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`.** Cross-reference it; do not change it. Architect \\u00a75 confirms it is already correct.\\n- **Do not remove or weaken the specialist-selection guidance.** Deleting the whole sentence would remove a true, still-needed statement. The task is a split, not a deletion.\\n- **Do not expand this into a general doctrine rewrite.** The file is 27 lines. Architect \\u00a77 defers a general \\\"all `dh:*` agents must declare a completion-report step\\\" gate to a separate item. Do not fold it in.\\n- **Do not add `TeamCreate`, `team-lead`, or `SendMessage`** to this file.\\n- **Do not add an enumerated destination list here.** Destination enumeration lives in `subagent-contract`; duplicating it would create two sources of truth for the same rule \\u2014 the exact failure mode this task exists to remove.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 `<dispatch_selection>` block only; a small, surgical diff.\\n\\nNo other files. No new files.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. The string `whose finding is its response` does not appear anywhere in `plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`.\\n2. The specialist-selection claim survives: a reader can still determine from the file that a dispatch handing over no dh operation may be run by any fitting specialist, dh roster or not.\\n3. The file cross-references `subagent-contract`'s `<result_destination>` clause as the sole authority on where a finding goes.\\n4. If `has nothing here to reach` survives, the surrounding prose makes it unambiguously mean \\\"no dh roster requirement\\\" and not \\\"no destination requirement.\\\"\\n5. The `<artifact_consumption>` block (lines 22-27) is byte-identical to its pre-change content.\\n6. The frontmatter is byte-identical to its pre-change content.\\n7. The second paragraph of `<dispatch_selection>` (lines 14-18, \\\"Once a dispatch hands over one...\\\") is byte-identical unless a change there is required to make the split read cleanly \\u2014 and if changed, the change preserves its full meaning.\\n8. No line containing `TeamCreate`, `team-lead`, or `SendMessage` was added.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `grep -c 'finding is its response' plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect `0`.\\n2. `grep -n 'dh roster or not' plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect a hit, confirming the specialist-selection claim survives.\\n3. `grep -n 'subagent-contract\\\\|result_destination' plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect at least one hit, confirming the cross-reference exists.\\n4. `git diff plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 confirm every hunk falls inside `<dispatch_selection>`; no hunk touches the frontmatter or `<artifact_consumption>`.\\n5. `git diff --stat plugins/development-harness/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` \\u2014 expect a small diff (single-digit cha